<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labourd</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lapurdi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Labourd (Basque Lapurdi) is one of the three historic Basque provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the Atlantic coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, with Ustaritz as its old seat of assembly. It was a viscounty and later part of the English duchy of Aquitaine before returning to the French crown in 1451, and it kept its own assembly and customs until the Revolution. In 1609 it suffered a notorious witch hunt led by Pierre de Lancre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labourd.jpg" alt="Labourd" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: An13sa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lapurdi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Labourd extends from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a>. Gipuzkoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> lie to the south, Lower Navarre to the east, and the Landes to the north. The province covers almost 900 square kilometres and is the most populous of the three French Basque provinces. Its main town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, but until the Revolution its capital was Ustaritz, 13 kilometres away, where local Basque leaders assembled. Other important towns are Biarritz, Anglet, Hendaye, Ciboure, Saint-Jean-de-Luz and Hasparren. Its people speak Basque, in the Lapurdian dialect, and the Gascon language also has a long tradition there, notably along the Adour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times Labourd was inhabited by the Tarbelli, an Aquitanian tribe whose fortified town of Lapurdum became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> and gave the region its name. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed part of the Duchy of Vasconia, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. Many authorities say Duke Sancho VI of Gascony ceded it to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a> around 1023, but the story has no basis in contemporary documents.</p><p>Richard the Lionheart ruled it directly between 1169 and 1199 and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> a charter in about 1174 or 1175. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-navarre">Berengaria of Navarre</a> in 1191 settled the border: Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> was annexed to Navarre while Labourd and Soule stayed in Angevin Aquitaine. In 1193 the legitimate viscounts, who had their seat at Ustaritz, sold their rights, and Ustaritz was the capital until the province was suppressed in 1790.</p><p>Labourd passed into French hands in 1451, just before the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, and was largely self-ruled as an autonomous French province until the Revolution. A major witch hunt led by the judge Pierre de Lancre, after feuds between merchants and nobility, ended with some 70 supposed sorginak burned at the stake. In 1793-1794, during the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, the Convention's repression brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportation</a> to the Landes, seizure of land, and the deaths of an estimated 1,600 civilians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> suppressed the provinces in 1790 and joined Labourd with Béarn in the département of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Basses-Pyrénées</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Labourd's coast played an important part in the early European exploitation of the Atlantic. Labourdin whalers gave the viscount the oil of the first animal they caught as early as 1059, and Basque whaling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> began in the 1530s. The rudder is first mentioned as steering in the Navarrese or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> fashion, and the development may have been specifically Labourdine.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> lost San Sebastián and Hondarribia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1200, a treaty made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> the port of Navarre for nearly three centuries. Traditional buildings have low roofs, half-timbering and red, white and green paint; the Villa Arnaga at Cambo-les-Bains, home of Edmond Rostand, is now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Labourd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bidache, on the Bidouze river between Béarn, Navarre and the <b>Labourd</b>, belonged to the Gramont family.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bidache">Principality of Bidache</a></li></ul></section>
